"""Slavic adjective suffixes: a negative dip on the n (or н) of an adjective ending.

In Czech, Slovak, Polish and Russian, adjectives and participles carry an "n" in their
endings and stems: -ný / -ní / -né / -ná (Czech, Slovak), -ny / -ne (Polish),
-ный / -ной / -ного / -ные / -ных (Russian). This component goes strongly negative on the
word piece that carries that n, when it continues a word (it is not the word's first
piece) and the word or the document so far is Slavic. It is strongest on Latin-script
n-endings (-n, -ne, -né, -ní, -ny, -ná, -nych, -ných) and on stems ending in a consonant
plus n (-tn-, -zn-, -dn-, -pn-), and about -1 on Russian adjective endings with н.
Everything else predicts 0. It cannot see the next token, so it cannot tell an adjective
from a noun that shares the same letters.

Slavic is judged from the current word (it contains a Czech/Slovak/Polish mark such as
č ř š ž ů ě ł ą, or any Cyrillic letter) or from the document so far (over 3% of its
letters are such marks or Cyrillic).
"""

import re
import unicodedata

READS = ()

SLAVIC_MARKS = set("čřšžůěťďňýľĺŕąęłśźżćń")
N_ENDINGS = {"n", "ne", "né", "ní", "ny", "ný", "ná", "na", "no", "ni", "nej", "nych", "ných"}
CONS_N = re.compile(r"^[bcdfghjklmnpqrstvwxzčšžřťďňľ]{1,2}n$")  # tn, zn, dn, pn, ...
